  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24&#8211;48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Trump's Aug 1 pause order on US military strikes against Iranian territory remained in effect through Aug 10. No CENTCOM official press release or authorized Pentagon briefing confirmed new airstrikes on Iranian sovereign territory in the Aug 7&#8211;11 window. Available evidence &#8212; including the absence of any authorized US government confirmation and continued Gulf diplomatic engagement by Saudi and other regional intermediaries &#8212; met the RESOLVE-NO criteria as of today's reporting cutoff. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Iranian Foreign Minister Araghchi articulated four named preconditions for any US contact on Aug 9, per IRNA and Tasnim coverage: US withdrawal of naval forces from the Strait of Hormuz, withdrawal of military forces from the broader region, full removal of all sanctions, and payment of war damages. The statement post-dates Qatar FM's Aug 4 characterization of indirect talks as "in very progressive stages" and is the more specifically dated and attributable Iranian MFA position available to this analysis. With a 7-day horizon through Aug 18 and four structurally unmeetable conditions on record, both triplet sources converge at 7%. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The Iran-Oman joint working group's Aug 9&#8211;11 Muscat session produced two documented deliverables: geographic routing coordinates for commercial transit through the Strait, and a dedicated real-time coordination center (hotline) for vessel-by-vessel passage requests. No TIER1-confirmed named service fee, fee formula, or formal governance-body document appeared in post-session reporting as of Aug 11. Mohsen Rezaei's Aug 9 appointment as SNSC Secretary &#8212; the institutional body responsible for ratifying any bilateral framework text &#8212; introduces an IRGC ratification layer; his institutional profile differs from his predecessor's documented operational flexibility on the Oman channel. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>F10 enters the board at 28%: the IRGC hostile-act probability for the Aug 12&#8211;Sep 7 window. The structural driver is Rezaei's SNSC appointment and his prior public characterization of Hormuz control as strategically superior to nuclear deterrence. Contributing factors are IRGC historical precedent for vessel interdiction during concurrent diplomatic openings and near-zero commercial transit (~2/day per IMF PortWatch data through Aug 2) across a 27-day window. The primary counterweight is Iran's active use of the Oman channel as the only functioning diplomatic track; a hostile act would compromise the only framework currently producing documented deliverables. → moved <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>Israeli polling through the Aug 9&#8211;11 window shows the right-wing bloc tracked at approximately 50&#8211;51 Knesset seats &#8212; consistent with prior survey series and 10 below the 61-seat coalition-formation threshold required by F6 resolution criteria. No new Israeli polling was published in the last 48 hours. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

