      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation (28%):</strong> Driven primarily by F10 at 28% &#8212; Rezaei's SNSC appointment and near-zero commercial transit create the primary escalation pathway via IRGC hostile act in the Strait across the Aug 12&#8211;Sep 7 window.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted (53%):</strong> The Oman channel continues producing operational deliverables (coordination center, routing coordinates) while the fee-structure gap and Araghchi's four-condition block keep the conflict in managed maritime stalemate through at least late August.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation (19%):</strong> Conditional on the Iran-Oman framework producing a named fee document before Aug 22 (F8, 15%) and Araghchi's four-condition block softening sufficiently for a mediated US-Iran session before Aug 18 (F3, 7%); neither alone reaches the de-escalation threshold.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; ~200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and state broadcaster IRIB framed the Aug 9&#8211;11 Muscat session as "operational implementation" of the Aug 6 corridor concept. Coverage in both outlets described the coordination center and geographic routing coordinates as concrete deliverables. The unresolved service-fee dispute &#8212; Iran's 5&#8211;7% cargo-value demand against Oman's proposed 3% voluntary cap &#8212; received no acknowledgment in any IRNA, Tasnim, or Press TV piece reviewed for this window. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan ran the headline "Hormuz Guardian Now Commands Security Council" on Aug 10, alongside an opinion piece reiterating that the strait falls within "Iran's strategic toolbox, not a multilateral commons." Fars covered the Rezaei appointment with extended biographical reference to his record as IRGC commander and his prior characterization of Hormuz control as a strategic priority superior to nuclear deterrence. These editorial choices are consistent with Rezaei's documented public statements and do not represent a departure from baseline Kayhan and Fars positioning on Hormuz affairs. → moved <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>Araghchi's Aug 9 four-condition statement was the lead foreign-policy story in IRNA on that date and ran in full on IRIB evening news. The conditions &#8212; naval withdrawal from the strait, regional military withdrawal, full sanctions removal, and war damages payment &#8212; were stated as prerequisites, not negotiating positions, in IRNA's framing. No Iranian state-media outlet presented a diplomatic pathway shorter than full US concession on all four conditions in the Aug 9&#8211;11 coverage window. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

